1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,330
Here is a game anyone can play.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,930
Three corners, one dot, and an ordinary die.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,020
Roll, and the dot jumps halfway towards the

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:10,650
corner the die picks.

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,250
Mark where it lands.

6
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:13,690
Then roll again.

7
00:00:14,164 --> 00:00:16,624
Before I run it fifty thousand times,

8
00:00:16,644 --> 00:00:17,534
make a guess.

9
00:00:17,844 --> 00:00:20,414
Will the dots end up all over the triangle,

10
00:00:20,524 --> 00:00:23,294
in some kind of pattern, or something else?

11
00:00:23,604 --> 00:00:25,054
Pause and pick one.

12
00:00:28,005 --> 00:00:29,455
I picked the first.

13
00:00:29,525 --> 00:00:30,415
Here we go.

14
00:00:34,434 --> 00:00:36,524
That is not what I expected.

15
00:00:36,914 --> 00:00:39,614
Every one of these dots was placed by chance,

16
00:00:39,634 --> 00:00:41,724
and not one of them is in a hole.

17
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:44,290
This is The Math Universe lab,

18
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:45,330
issue two.

19
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:48,260
Four experiments, one open problem,

20
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,370
and a board you can play with yourself.

21
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:51,970
Let us find out why.

22
00:00:53,095 --> 00:00:55,475
My first thought was that I had simply been

23
00:00:55,495 --> 00:00:56,865
lucky with the start.

24
00:00:57,095 --> 00:00:58,865
The dot began on a corner.

25
00:00:59,015 --> 00:01:00,945
So let us be unfair to it.

26
00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:03,555
I am moving the starting dot into the middle

27
00:01:03,575 --> 00:01:04,865
of the biggest hole.

28
00:01:06,402 --> 00:01:07,492
Roll once.

29
00:01:07,682 --> 00:01:09,052
It lands in a hole.

30
00:01:09,362 --> 00:01:10,252
Roll again.

31
00:01:10,402 --> 00:01:12,332
Another hole, a smaller one.

32
00:01:12,642 --> 00:01:15,372
So a dot can land in a hole after all.

33
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:16,572
Keep rolling.

34
00:01:16,961 --> 00:01:18,491
Now watch the readout.

35
00:01:18,721 --> 00:01:21,211
However far the dot is from the triangle,

36
00:01:21,521 --> 00:01:23,771
every jump halves that distance.

37
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:27,021
After ten jumps it is a thousand times closer

38
00:01:27,041 --> 00:01:28,171
than where it began.

39
00:01:28,481 --> 00:01:31,371
On this board, that is less than a pixel.

40
00:01:31,779 --> 00:01:34,189
So I was asking the wrong question.

41
00:01:34,419 --> 00:01:36,749
A random dot can land in a hole.

42
00:01:36,979 --> 00:01:40,269
What it cannot do is stay away from the triangle.

43
00:01:40,745 --> 00:01:42,915
Here is the version I like best.

44
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:46,275
A hundred dots, started all over the board,

45
00:01:46,345 --> 00:01:48,115
each with its own rolls.

46
00:01:48,345 --> 00:01:51,235
Watch them all fall onto the same shape.

47
00:01:54,194 --> 00:01:56,124
The die decides the route.

48
00:01:56,194 --> 00:01:58,734
It has no say at all in where the dots end

49
00:01:58,754 --> 00:01:59,244
up.

50
00:02:00,283 --> 00:02:02,053
Look at this line on the board:

51
00:02:02,443 --> 00:02:04,853
last rolls, newest first.

52
00:02:05,163 --> 00:02:07,143
I put it there because it turns out to be

53
00:02:07,163 --> 00:02:08,533
the dot's address.

54
00:02:08,935 --> 00:02:12,035
Split the triangle into three half-size corner

55
00:02:12,055 --> 00:02:12,945
triangles.

56
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:16,515
Call them A, B and C, and leave the middle

57
00:02:16,535 --> 00:02:17,185
out.

58
00:02:17,575 --> 00:02:20,145
Jump towards A, and wherever you were,

59
00:02:20,215 --> 00:02:21,905
you land inside A.

60
00:02:22,295 --> 00:02:25,315
Then jump towards B, and you land inside the

61
00:02:25,335 --> 00:02:27,105
A-shaped corner of B.

62
00:02:27,415 --> 00:02:28,945
Call it BA.

63
00:02:29,335 --> 00:02:31,835
Every roll adds one letter to the front of

64
00:02:31,855 --> 00:02:32,705
the address.

65
00:02:33,105 --> 00:02:36,645
So after ten rolls, the dot's address is just

66
00:02:36,665 --> 00:02:38,235
its last ten rolls.

67
00:02:38,465 --> 00:02:41,515
And a hole is a place no address leads to,

68
00:02:41,825 --> 00:02:44,285
because every letter means a jump into a corner

69
00:02:44,305 --> 00:02:47,115
triangle, and the middle is never one of them.

70
00:02:47,465 --> 00:02:49,915
The die only ever chooses letters.

71
00:02:50,225 --> 00:02:52,875
That is why the dots never land in a hole.

72
00:02:53,355 --> 00:02:55,375
The colours on the board have been saying

73
00:02:55,395 --> 00:02:56,405
this all along.

74
00:02:56,715 --> 00:02:59,365
Each dot is coloured by its last roll,

75
00:02:59,515 --> 00:03:01,765
so the three colours never mix.

76
00:03:02,815 --> 00:03:05,715
If the die is innocent, cheating with it should

77
00:03:05,735 --> 00:03:07,545
not change anything important.

78
00:03:07,895 --> 00:03:09,305
So I am loading it.

79
00:03:09,655 --> 00:03:12,155
Corner one now comes up nine times out of

80
00:03:12,175 --> 00:03:12,745
ten.

81
00:03:13,139 --> 00:03:14,829
Pause and guess again.

82
00:03:15,139 --> 00:03:18,029
A lopsided triangle, a different shape,

83
00:03:18,139 --> 00:03:19,469
or the same shape?

84
00:03:22,487 --> 00:03:25,697
It starts lopsided: crowded at the top,

85
00:03:25,847 --> 00:03:27,217
starved at the bottom.

86
00:03:27,527 --> 00:03:29,387
But look at where the dots are allowed to

87
00:03:29,407 --> 00:03:29,777
be.

88
00:03:30,007 --> 00:03:31,537
Not one in a hole.

89
00:03:31,847 --> 00:03:33,987
Leave it running, and the starved corners

90
00:03:34,007 --> 00:03:38,097
fill in, slowly, into exactly the same triangle.

91
00:03:38,584 --> 00:03:42,274
A crooked die changes how fast each part appears.

92
00:03:42,504 --> 00:03:44,354
It cannot change the shape.

93
00:03:45,405 --> 00:03:48,025
Which means the die was never drawing this

94
00:03:48,045 --> 00:03:48,695
picture.

95
00:03:48,925 --> 00:03:51,335
So let us throw it away completely.

96
00:03:51,811 --> 00:03:54,381
Instead of one dot taking one jump,

97
00:03:54,571 --> 00:03:57,231
take a whole picture and make all three jumps

98
00:03:57,251 --> 00:03:58,141
at once.

99
00:03:58,371 --> 00:04:01,261
A square becomes three half-size squares.

100
00:04:01,411 --> 00:04:02,381
Then nine.

101
00:04:02,771 --> 00:04:05,071
Eight rounds later there are six and a half

102
00:04:05,091 --> 00:04:07,751
thousand tiny squares, and they have arranged

103
00:04:07,771 --> 00:04:09,581
themselves into the triangle.

104
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:12,590
Start from a circle, or a face,

105
00:04:12,610 --> 00:04:14,700
and you end up in the same place.

106
00:04:15,010 --> 00:04:18,230
In 1981, John Hutchinson proved that there

107
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:20,990
is exactly one shape these three rules leave

108
00:04:21,010 --> 00:04:24,110
unchanged, and every starting picture is pulled

109
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:25,020
towards it.

110
00:04:25,330 --> 00:04:27,900
The die is just a quick way of visiting it.

111
00:04:28,289 --> 00:04:30,219
The shape itself is older.

112
00:04:30,529 --> 00:04:33,309
The Polish mathematician Sierpinski described

113
00:04:33,329 --> 00:04:35,019
it in 1915.

114
00:04:35,449 --> 00:04:38,109
Drawing it with a die comes from Michael Barnsley's

115
00:04:38,129 --> 00:04:40,939
1988 book Fractals Everywhere,

116
00:04:41,129 --> 00:04:43,229
and it has been called the chaos game ever

117
00:04:43,249 --> 00:04:43,899
since.

118
00:04:44,958 --> 00:04:46,088
Back to the board.

119
00:04:46,318 --> 00:04:48,088
If the rule makes the shape,

120
00:04:48,398 --> 00:04:50,408
changing the rule should change the shape.

121
00:04:50,758 --> 00:04:52,888
The obvious knob is the jump.

122
00:04:53,158 --> 00:04:54,248
Why halfway?

123
00:04:54,734 --> 00:04:55,784
Guess first.

124
00:04:56,014 --> 00:04:58,344
If I jump only a third of the way,

125
00:04:58,494 --> 00:05:00,264
does the triangle fill in,

126
00:05:00,334 --> 00:05:01,624
or fall apart?

127
00:05:04,656 --> 00:05:07,543
A third: the three copies are too big.

128
00:05:07,563 --> 00:05:10,506
They overlap, and the holes close up.

129
00:05:10,976 --> 00:05:14,156
Now two thirds: the copies are too small to

130
00:05:14,176 --> 00:05:17,306
touch, and the triangle crumbles into dust.

131
00:05:17,776 --> 00:05:20,476
Halfway is exactly where the three copies

132
00:05:20,496 --> 00:05:21,546
just touch.

133
00:05:21,955 --> 00:05:24,765
Three copies, each half as wide.

134
00:05:25,075 --> 00:05:27,615
That gives the triangle a dimension of about

135
00:05:27,635 --> 00:05:29,405
one point five eight five:

136
00:05:29,715 --> 00:05:32,525
more than a line, less than a filled sheet.

137
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:34,410
Corners matter too.

138
00:05:34,573 --> 00:05:37,633
Four corners at halfway give no fractal at

139
00:05:37,653 --> 00:05:40,383
all; the dots simply fill the square.

140
00:05:40,773 --> 00:05:43,023
Add the four midpoints of the sides,

141
00:05:43,093 --> 00:05:46,143
jump two thirds, and a different fractal appears:

142
00:05:46,333 --> 00:05:47,903
the Sierpinski carpet.

143
00:05:48,377 --> 00:05:50,797
And a rule does not have to be a jump towards

144
00:05:50,817 --> 00:05:51,587
a corner.

145
00:05:51,817 --> 00:05:53,987
A rule can shrink, squash,

146
00:05:54,137 --> 00:05:55,347
turn and slide.

147
00:05:55,697 --> 00:05:58,437
Barnsley's most famous example uses four of

148
00:05:58,457 --> 00:05:58,867
them.

149
00:06:01,904 --> 00:06:04,874
One is chosen eighty-five times in a hundred,

150
00:06:05,024 --> 00:06:07,564
and builds the fern out of a slightly smaller

151
00:06:07,584 --> 00:06:08,794
copy of itself.

152
00:06:09,184 --> 00:06:11,754
Another is chosen just once in a hundred,

153
00:06:11,904 --> 00:06:14,234
and that one rule draws the stem.

154
00:06:14,624 --> 00:06:17,084
Twenty-eight numbers, and a shape that looks

155
00:06:17,104 --> 00:06:17,914
alive.

156
00:06:18,965 --> 00:06:20,975
And this is not only a game.

157
00:06:21,125 --> 00:06:25,105
In 1996 a team in Barcelona built the triangle

158
00:06:25,125 --> 00:06:26,255
as an antenna.

159
00:06:26,485 --> 00:06:29,215
Because it contains smaller copies of itself,

160
00:06:29,365 --> 00:06:32,495
one small antenna can work on several bands.

161
00:06:32,894 --> 00:06:34,794
In 1990, H.

162
00:06:34,814 --> 00:06:35,034
J.

163
00:06:35,054 --> 00:06:37,784
Jeffrey played the chaos game with DNA.

164
00:06:38,174 --> 00:06:41,944
Put A, C, G and T at the corners of a square,

165
00:06:42,014 --> 00:06:43,754
and for each letter of a gene,

166
00:06:43,774 --> 00:06:46,024
jump halfway towards that corner.

167
00:06:46,334 --> 00:06:49,144
A random sequence fills the square evenly.

168
00:06:49,534 --> 00:06:52,314
Real genes leave gaps, and the gaps are a

169
00:06:52,334 --> 00:06:53,224
fingerprint.

170
00:06:53,534 --> 00:06:55,914
Here is a made-up sequence in which one pair

171
00:06:55,934 --> 00:06:57,304
of letters is rare.

172
00:06:57,534 --> 00:06:59,384
The gap shows it at once.

173
00:07:00,430 --> 00:07:02,840
Now the part I have not solved for you.

174
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:05,080
Investigation number three.

175
00:07:05,390 --> 00:07:07,960
Choose the hexagon: six corners,

176
00:07:08,030 --> 00:07:08,840
one die.

177
00:07:09,070 --> 00:07:11,960
At halfway, the six copies overlap,

178
00:07:11,990 --> 00:07:13,400
and you can see the blur.

179
00:07:13,889 --> 00:07:17,419
So what jump makes the six copies just touch,

180
00:07:17,489 --> 00:07:19,579
with no gap and no overlap?

181
00:07:19,889 --> 00:07:22,059
Watch, I will nudge the slider.

182
00:07:22,209 --> 00:07:23,579
It is getting sharper.

183
00:07:23,889 --> 00:07:26,699
Somewhere past here it snaps into place,

184
00:07:26,809 --> 00:07:28,459
and this is where I stop.

185
00:07:29,913 --> 00:07:32,163
Part two: the pentagon.

186
00:07:32,393 --> 00:07:35,363
Five corners, and you roll again on a six.

187
00:07:35,593 --> 00:07:36,563
Same question.

188
00:07:36,713 --> 00:07:39,253
Work it out, on paper or on the board,

189
00:07:39,273 --> 00:07:42,083
and a very famous number is waiting for you.

190
00:07:42,562 --> 00:07:44,572
Share what you find in the comments,

191
00:07:44,642 --> 00:07:47,292
even a guess or a half-finished idea.

192
00:07:47,602 --> 00:07:50,022
Next Thursday, the lab opens with what you

193
00:07:50,042 --> 00:07:50,812
discovered.

194
00:07:50,962 --> 00:07:53,692
No names, unless you would like yours mentioned.

195
00:07:54,738 --> 00:07:57,118
And Investigations one and two,

196
00:07:57,138 --> 00:07:59,598
from the coffee cup, are still open until

197
00:07:59,618 --> 00:08:01,308
the thirtieth of October.

198
00:08:01,719 --> 00:08:05,009
Before you go, I would love to know two things.

199
00:08:05,319 --> 00:08:07,649
Which experiment surprised you most?

200
00:08:07,839 --> 00:08:09,729
And what would you try next?

201
00:08:10,039 --> 00:08:12,849
Here is one I made up: four corners,

202
00:08:12,999 --> 00:08:14,769
never the same corner twice.

203
00:08:14,999 --> 00:08:17,246
I did not know what it would draw until I

204
00:08:17,266 --> 00:08:17,889
ran it.

205
00:08:20,837 --> 00:08:22,447
Now it is your turn.

206
00:08:22,597 --> 00:08:24,497
The board is free, and the link is in the

207
00:08:24,517 --> 00:08:25,407
description.

208
00:08:25,637 --> 00:08:28,367
Place your own corners, change the jump,

209
00:08:28,437 --> 00:08:30,927
invent a rule, and tell me what appeared.

210
00:08:31,157 --> 00:08:32,767
I read every comment.
